The actual letters do not matter here. What matters is when each letter appeared before. I keep the last position for characters in both strings. If the two current characters have different previous positions, their patterns do not match.

The stored position uses `i + 1`, because zero means that the character has not appeared yet.

For `s = "paper"` and `t = "title"`, the repeated `p` and `t` both appear again at the same relative point, so the pattern is accepted.

Important edge cases are different lengths and empty strings. Different lengths return `False`, while two empty strings return `True` because the loop has nothing that conflicts.

```python
class Solution:
    def isomorphicString(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:
        if len(s) != len(t):
            return False

        last_s = {}
        last_t = {}

        for i, (char_s, char_t) in enumerate(zip(s, t)):
            previous_s = last_s.get(char_s, 0)
            previous_t = last_t.get(char_t, 0)

            if previous_s != previous_t:
                return False

            last_s[char_s] = i + 1
            last_t[char_t] = i + 1

        return True
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` average time.
Space complexity: `O(k)` for the saved character positions.

I kept this version close to the definition. For every pair of positions, it checks whether equal characters in `s` also have equal characters in `t`, and the other way around. If either rule breaks, the strings are not valid.

Dry run with `s = "egg"` and `t = "add"`:
- The two `g` characters match the two `d` characters at the same positions.
- `e` and `a` appear once, so there is no conflict.
- The function returns `true`.

This does not use maps, but it checks earlier positions again for each character, so it is slower than the one-pass versions.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isomorphicString(string s, string t) {
        if (s.size() != t.size()) {
            return false;
        }

        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                if (s[i] == s[j] && t[i] != t[j]) {
                    return false;
                }
                if (t[i] == t[j] && s[i] != s[j]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This version keeps one map from `s` to `t` and a set of target characters that are already taken. The set is needed for cases like `s = "ab"` and `t = "cc"`, where the forward map alone would accept two different source characters using the same target.

When a source character is seen again, its old target must match. When it is new, its target must not already be in the set.

The input strings are only read and are not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isomorphicString(string s, string t) {
        if (s.size() != t.size()) {
            return false;
        }

        unordered_map<char, char> mapping;
        unordered_set<char> usedTargets;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(s.size()); ++i) {
            char source = s[i];
            char target = t[i];

            auto found = mapping.find(source);
            if (found != mapping.end()) {
                if (found->second != target) {
                    return false;
                }
            } else {
                if (usedTargets.count(target) != 0) {
                    return false;
                }
                mapping[source] = target;
                usedTargets.insert(target);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` average time with hash table operations.
Space complexity: `O(k)`, based on the number of different characters.

A character from `s` must always point to the same character in `t`. Also, two different characters from `s` cannot point to one character in `t`. Two maps make both checks direct.

The length test is done first, so the loop never reads past the shorter string. The maps only store characters that have appeared so far.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isomorphicString(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        java.util.HashMap<Character, Character> forward = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        java.util.HashMap<Character, Character> backward = new java.util.HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char from = s.charAt(i);
            char to = t.charAt(i);

            if (forward.containsKey(from) && forward.get(from) != to) {
                return false;
            }
            if (backward.containsKey(to) && backward.get(to) != from) {
                return false;
            }

            forward.put(from, to);
            backward.put(to, from);
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of different characters stored.

## Intuition
Imagine you're decoding a secret cipher. Each letter in the original message maps to exactly one letter in the coded version — and crucially, no two different letters can map to the same coded letter.
That's exactly what isomorphic strings are about.

"egg" → "add" ✅   (e→a, g→d)
"foo" → "bar" ❌   (o can't map to both a and r)
"paper" → "title" ✅   (p→t, a→i, e→l, r→e)

The key insight: the structure of repetitions must match perfectly in both strings.

## Approach
A one-way map isn't enough. Consider "ab" → "aa":

a→a and b→a both look fine left-to-right...
...but a in t can't map back to both a and b in s!

So we maintain two hash maps simultaneously:
```
s_t : s → t  (each char in s maps to exactly one char in t)
t_s : t → s  (each char in t maps to exactly one char in s)
```

At each index i, we check:

If s[i] already has a mapping → it must point to t[i]
If t[i] already has a mapping → it must point back to s[i]

If either check fails, the strings aren't isomorphic. Otherwise, we record both mappings and move on.

